        }
      ],
      "body": {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Chemical reactions involve the breaking and making of chemical bonds. When a bond forms between two atoms energy is released, and the same amount is absorbed when the bond is broken to give neutral atoms."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "tone": "definition",
            "title": "Bond dissociation energy",
            "text": "The average amount of energy required to break (dissociate) one mole of a particular bond in a substance, denoted E."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The bond energy of a particular bond in a particular substance is its exact bond energy. Bonds between the same pair of atoms usually have different values in different compounds, because the bond energy is affected by the other atoms in the molecule: C-C bonds have values of about 350 to 380 kJ per mol, 376 in ethane, 356 in propane and 352 in butane. Each of these is an exact bond energy; averaging all the C-C values in different molecules gives the average C-C bond energy. In practice average bond energies are used instead of exact ones."
          },
